e63fc731dd87fd10563dd380fd4431553ed15492c7f9447"},"seconds":12.742703437805176,"seed":725857289332254694,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the points of intersection of the parabolas defined by the equations \\(y = -x^2 - x + 1\\) and \\(y = 2x^2 - 1\\), we set the equations equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n-x^2 - x + 1 = 2x^2 - 1\n\\]\n\nNext, we rearrange the equation to bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\n-x^2 - x + 1 - 2x^2 + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\n\\[\n-3x^2 - x + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we use the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = -3\\), \\(b = -1\\), and \\(c = 2\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-(-1) \\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^2 - 4(-3)(2)}}{2(-3)}\n\\]\n\nSimplify inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 24}}{-6}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{25}}{-6}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1 \\pm 5}{-6}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1 + 5}{-6} = \\frac{6}{-6} = -1\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1 - 5}{-6} = \\frac{-4}{-6} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the points of intersection occur at \\(x = -1\\) and \\(x = \\frac{2}{3}\\). Given that \\(c \\ge a\\), we have \\(c = \\frac{2}{3}\\) and \\(a = -1\\).\n\nTo find \\(c - a\\):\n\n\\[\nc - a = \\frac{2}{3} - (-1) = \\frac{2}{3} + 1 = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{3}{3} = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{5}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:397"}
